00:14:29,760 --> 00:14:33,360
And then nine times out of ten, you wake up the next day and you're like, that was idiotic.

212
00:14:33,680 --> 00:14:34,160
Delete that.

213
00:14:34,640 --> 00:14:35,200
Like, wow.

214
00:14:35,600 --> 00:14:36,480
That was really dumb.

215
00:14:36,880 --> 00:14:42,800
And effectively what I do is I wait until it stays around really long time.

216
00:14:44,240 --> 00:14:51,520
And, you know, and when you have it in the back of your mind, like something will go by Twitter or something will happen in a daily conversation or you'll see someone using a weather app.

217
00:14:51,840 --> 00:14:53,680
And I'll be like, hey, what do you think about this?

218
00:14:53,840 --> 00:14:55,200
Like, what if it worked like this?

219
00:14:55,520 --> 00:14:57,360
Or why is no one doing it like this?

220
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:01,920
So at any given time, there's whatever, three or four of those things that I'm just thinking about.

221
00:15:02,720 --> 00:15:10,800
And then as things come, as things happen, or I think like, oh, that anecdote that I just got shows that this actually would work or wouldn't work.

222
00:15:10,880 --> 00:15:13,760
And I'll kind of just write it in the note for that idea.

223
00:15:14,240 --> 00:15:22,800
And then this is kind of the sad part, to me at least, is it takes like three or four years of that thing sticking around for me to be like, okay, I'll do that.

224
00:15:23,120 --> 00:15:24,880
And that's how Weatherline happened.

225
00:15:25,040 --> 00:15:26,080
That's how Flighty happened.

226
00:15:26,480 --> 00:15:26,880
That's funny.

227
00:15:27,120 --> 00:15:27,200
Yeah.

228
00:15:28,320 --> 00:15:28,960
I'm similar.

229
00:15:30,080 --> 00:15:34,560
I have a note that is just ideas, which is literally anything.

230
00:15:35,040 --> 00:15:39,360
And it's an outline view of the world really generic things.

231
00:15:39,680 --> 00:15:46,560
And I do the same thing where it's like I just put, you know, like weather app and then I just throw ideas in there over time.

232
00:15:47,040 --> 00:15:56,080
And if it grows big enough because I just keep thinking about things that it looks really silly, then it graduates to its own note and then it's like it kind of grows over time.

233
00:15:56,240 --> 00:16:00,080
And eventually, either I'm like, okay, I just can't do this well.

234
00:16:00,320 --> 00:16:02,480
And then it just lives forever, which there are a couple that are like that.

235
00:16:02,640 --> 00:16:05,760
And I still, to this day, will wake up in the middle of the night and add a new idea to it.

236
00:16:06,560 --> 00:16:12,480
Or if I'm like, I need a new project, I'll kind of look through that list and maybe pick one that seems like it's easy or something.

237
00:16:13,520 --> 00:16:16,000
It sounds like a lot of people have a similar sort of thing.

238
00:16:16,240 --> 00:16:18,640
It's like you have to get these ideas out of your head somehow.

239
00:16:18,880 --> 00:16:18,960
Yeah.

240
00:16:19,520 --> 00:16:24,960
Doesn't it feel, I mean, for me, it feels so depressing that it takes me so long to be like, okay, I'll do that one.

241
00:16:25,520 --> 00:16:26,800
It takes like three or four years.

242
00:16:27,360 --> 00:16:37,280
I can say that the very first one I've had, I had this was way before I was doing iOS development, but when I first switched to iOS, actually, I may have been, I was on Android actually when this started.

243
00:16:37,520 --> 00:16:38,880
So it started as an Android idea.

244
00:16:39,520 --> 00:16:43,200
To this day, I still add things to it, and I've never picked it up.

245
00:16:45,040 --> 00:16:55,680
And it's like, it's like a decent idea, and I've thought about trying to convince friends to do it because I'm like, I just want this to exist, but I can't quite get to the point where I'll jump into it.

246
00:16:55,840 --> 00:16:57,280
So I know exactly what you're talking about.

247
00:16:57,840 --> 00:16:59,760
Yeah, it's a good, it's a good filter.

248
00:17:00,080 --> 00:17:07,440
And I'm sure you know this feeling too, but like, you'll see a new company or a new product and you're like, Ooh, that sounds like what my note app is.

249
00:17:07,520 --> 00:17:12,320
And you open it up and you're like, part, sometimes your part of you is like, Man, I hope it's exactly the same thing.

250
00:17:12,640 --> 00:17:13,840
And I hope it goes super well.

251
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:15,600
And I'll be like, Damn it, I should have done that.

252
00:17:15,680 --> 00:17:16,240
That was a great idea.

253
00:17:16,480 --> 00:17:16,960
Yeah, exactly.

254
00:17:17,040 --> 00:17:19,040
Or sometimes you're like, I hope it's exactly the same thing.

255
00:17:19,120 --> 00:17:22,480
And it just burns down in flames so that I knew that I made the right decision not doing it.

256
00:17:22,640 --> 00:17:26,160
And it's just a weird, like, I don't know how my mind goes one way or the other.

257
00:17:26,480 --> 00:17:36,800
Yeah, or you'll see it and you if it's one of those ideas that you still think you're going to do in your head and you open up the website and then you see like, oh, they missed that one important thing.

258
00:17:37,040 --> 00:17:37,680
Yeah, good deal.

259
00:17:37,760 --> 00:17:38,640
Like, I'm still good.

260
00:17:39,360 --> 00:17:40,240
The hope is still alive.

261
00:17:40,640 --> 00:17:40,960
Exactly.

262
00:17:41,920 --> 00:17:47,680
It's such, I don't know what it is, naivety or ignorance of like, yeah, that one product thing is going to change everything.

263
00:17:48,160 --> 00:17:50,160
Or just overwhelming confidence.

264
00:17:50,400 --> 00:17:50,480
Yeah.

265
00:17:51,120 --> 00:17:52,080
I've got it all figured out.

266
00:17:52,400 --> 00:17:55,600
Before you actually had to try and do it for real, it seems like it's so easy.

267
00:17:55,760 --> 00:17:55,840
Yeah.

268
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:56,160
No.

269
00:17:56,400 --> 00:18:00,560
I haven't tried to build anything and no users have interacted with it and told me, oh, this is a stupid idea, but man.

270
00:18:00,880 --> 00:18:02,560
man, this thing on paper is genius.

271
00:18:03,120 --> 00:18:03,840
Yeah, exactly.

272
00:18:05,360 --> 00:18:12,000
Okay, so you at some point decided you're going to give yourself six months to try and make Weatherline happen.

273
00:18:12,480 --> 00:18:17,360
But at this point you already knew coding is not something that you want to be doing.

274
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:19,520
So what was your next step?

275
00:18:19,680 --> 00:18:21,520
Because this is something I've always been curious about.

276
00:18:21,840 --> 00:18:23,520
Yeah, people are always curious about this.

277
00:18:23,760 --> 00:18:29,280
So I gave myself six months and I set the bar, I don't know why, I don't know how.

278
00:18:29,440 --> 00:18:41,120
I set the bar as if I can get this app, if I make this thing and it, I can get it into the top ten apps in the entire app App Store, then that means I'm decent at this and I can keep going.

279
00:18:41,440 --> 00:18:45,920
And this was early App Store days where those lists were like, hugely important, right?

280
00:18:46,320 --> 00:18:46,880
Correct, yeah.

281
00:18:47,360 --> 00:18:54,160
And everyone was looking at them all kind of all the time because popular apps would come out and they're always paid upfront because that's all that was.

282
00:18:54,800 --> 00:19:11,280
So a good popular app that had like a potential, I would say, kind of almost always had that initial spike upfront and kind of how big the spike was pretty decent indicator of how well things were going.

283
00:19:11,680 --> 00:19:13,600
So that was the barrier., for some reason.

284
00:19:13,840 --> 00:19:18,880
So what I did in Hexed, in hindsight, was pretty smart, but I was just stumbling around like everyone.

285
00:19:19,920 --> 00:19:25,440
So all I knew was people that I followed on Twitter who I thought made great products and they're great products.

286
00:19:25,520 --> 00:19:33,840
And I would just poke around in them all day long and learn the intricacies of it and the UX patterns, and I just thought it was so interesting that I was playing around with them.

287
00:19:34,560 --> 00:19:39,120
So I knew Tap Bots guys, Tweedy guys, I knew all these people existing.

288
00:19:39,360 --> 00:19:48,720
So what I did was I used some random site and downloaded a list of everyone who followed them and I then I cross referenced them all.

289
00:19:49,520 --> 00:20:00,400
So it's like, okay, anyone who follows the same people that I'm following must like their work and kind of have the same ethos and design principles and be interested in this world.

290
00:20:01,360 --> 00:20:10,000
So combine all those, anyone who's on all of those lists, start looking through profiles and find people who are engineers and start talking to them.

291
00:20:10,240 --> 00:20:12,800
Wow, just cold, cold, I guess DMing.

292
00:20:12,960 --> 00:20:14,320
Did Twitter have DMing at that time?

293
00:20:15,040 --> 00:20:15,920
I don't even remember.

294
00:20:16,320 --> 00:20:24,960
But what I would do, so it took me a long time, I think like a lot of people, but I really finally got over the idea of a gameplay.

295
00:20:24,960 --> 00:20:31,760
You're not going to tell an engineer, hey, here's the app I want to build, and they're going to go steal it and build it and become billionaires, and you're going to be stuck there with nothing.

296
00:20:32,480 --> 00:20:38,560
You were afraid, like, yeah, if you showed it to them that they were just going to be like, We'll screw you and go build it themselves.

297
00:20:38,960 --> 00:20:39,120
Right.

298
00:20:39,440 --> 00:20:39,520
Yeah.

299
00:20:39,840 --> 00:20:51,520
So for anyone else who thinks that kind of the way right now, the way I think about that and the way that I think it's true is, it takes a lot of heart and a lot of time and a lot of soul to build these things.

300
00:20:52,320 --> 00:20:55,360
And everyone has had someone come up to them and say, Oh, I have an idea for an app.

301
00:20:55,600 --> 00:20:56,560
Like, what about this?

302
00:20:57,040 --> 00:21:00,160
And 99 times out of 100, it's idiotic.

303
00:21:02,800 --> 00:21:14,080
And probably some of those 99 were decent ideas, you just don't care and it's not right in your heart and it's not you're not willing to put the time and the grinding into building it.

304
00:21:14,320 --> 00:21:18,080
So just nobody cares about your idea.

305
00:21:18,800 --> 00:21:31,120
Yeah, the con artist type people who are going to turn out a quick clone, the reason that's lucrative is because they're piggybacking off of all the marketing and work that the other person did.

306
00:21:31,840 --> 00:21:35,680
But building it raw from scratch doesn't fit because it takes so much work to do that.

307
00:21:35,840 --> 00:21:36,400
It's like a different.

308
00:21:37,200 --> 00:21:41,280
type of person or I don't know what the right word is there, but you know what I mean?

309
00:21:41,360 --> 00:21:42,000
Like, yeah.

310
00:21:42,400 --> 00:21:48,160
The risk of someone just quickly cashing in on an idea is pretty low if it's just at the idea phase, I feel like.

311
00:21:48,400 --> 00:21:48,720
Exactly.

312
00:21:48,880 --> 00:21:48,960
Yeah.

313
00:21:49,280 --> 00:21:56,720
I mean, it's hard enough to get someone who, like your brother or your sister or your mom to understand the app you're talking about and be like, yeah, I would use that.

314
00:21:56,880 --> 00:22:00,080
Like, you're not going to convince someone to pour six months of their life into building it.

315
00:22:00,160 --> 00:22:00,480
Like, it.

316
00:22:01,760 --> 00:22:04,960
So I got over that and I basically made what I call a one sheet.

317
00:22:05,920 --> 00:22:11,040
There's a one screen pencil drawn in the middle of a 8.5 by 11 sheet of paper.

318
00:22:11,200 --> 00:22:28,160
It was like, here's the main screen of the app, and then a bunch of arrows pointing towards different things saying what they did and like why it was unique, and then a couple of bullet points out on the sideline of, you know, here's where I think we can get this data, here's who I had in mind to do this design for stuff.

319
00:22:28,800 --> 00:22:33,600
So I had it all kind of pretty condensed, and I just started sharing that with engineers.

320
00:22:33,840 --> 00:22:41,120
And so this was a design and sort of a business plan then too, I guess, because you had already looked up data providers and costs and stuff like that.

321
00:22:41,520 --> 00:22:42,560
Yeah, I guess that is true.

322
00:22:42,720 --> 00:22:43,120
That's fair.

323
00:22:43,600 --> 00:22:48,880
The good news is back at that time it was super obvious to use Dark Skies data, so it was like everyone used that.

324
00:22:49,520 --> 00:22:57,200
And the business model was a choice of one, uh, which was paid upfront or I guess totally free with, I guess they had ads, but that wasn't going to.

325
00:22:57,680 --> 00:22:57,920
Right.

326
00:22:58,320 --> 00:22:58,400
Yeah.

327
00:22:58,640 --> 00:23:01,280
And, oh man, it's funny how much I know about the weather world now.

328
00:23:01,600 --> 00:23:03,440
Just, it's incredible amount.

329
00:23:03,600 --> 00:23:05,520
It's like, dude, you've made every single wrong choice.

330
00:23:05,680 --> 00:23:07,520
But back then it was, you know, it was the only choice.

331
00:23:08,160 --> 00:23:10,240
So that was how I did it, I guess.

332
00:23:10,480 --> 00:23:14,080
Um, I only ended up having to send it to like three or four engineers, I think, before.

333
00:23:14,640 --> 00:23:22,640
And what I would do is basically look through their profiles and find people who, you know, I didn't want to see someone who said like, whatever, engineer at Path or like some amazing app.

334
00:23:22,880 --> 00:23:24,880
Like, well, I'm not going to convince that person to build it.

335
00:23:24,880 --> 00:23:27,600
I had to convince that person to build this thing for me or to like do contract work.

336
00:23:27,840 --> 00:23:39,920
I had to find someone who, you know, I'd look through their tweets, you kinda get a sense of who they are and like if they're interacting with people and just started asking them, hey, I'm doing this, would you be interested in talking about it more?

337
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:42,400
And I probably got their email and then sent it over via email.

338
00:23:42,800 --> 00:23:43,200
Interesting.

339
00:23:43,600 --> 00:23:43,680
Okay.

340
00:23:44,400 --> 00:23:46,720
So eventually you got someone.

341
00:23:47,360 --> 00:23:50,080
I don't know what the team was that you had for Weatherline, at least not upfront.

342
00:23:50,240 --> 00:23:52,080
Yeah, it was a guy named, a guy named Marcia.

343
00:23:52,400 --> 00:23:55,760
Who was from Romania or Belarus, one of the two.

344
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:56,720
Super great guy.

345
00:23:56,880 --> 00:23:58,000
And I literally just contacted.

346
00:23:58,160 --> 00:24:00,880
I was like, here's the amount of money I will give you.

347
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:38,960
The best advice on the face of the earth, if only you knew this, you'd be successful.

348
00:24:40,560 --> 00:24:45,280
It's the people who start plus wanting your ideas or saying, ah, yeah, I get that.

349
00:24:45,440 --> 00:24:46,640
Like, I understand that.

350
00:24:46,800 --> 00:24:47,200
What about this?

351
00:24:47,440 --> 00:24:48,160
Have you thought about that?

352
00:24:48,880 --> 00:25:00,000
They are, understand what you're trying to do, like, intuitively, like, instinctively, like in themselves, and are excited enough about it to start adding on top of it.

353
00:25:00,400 --> 00:25:02,640
Which isn't something you can fake to your fear.

354
00:25:02,800 --> 00:25:04,000
It's like, yeah.

355
00:25:04,240 --> 00:25:07,200
I think the point of that is the person gets it.

356
00:25:07,440 --> 00:25:11,040
They're not just taking the stack of requirements and implementing those requirements.

357
00:25:11,280 --> 00:25:18,560
They're like, I get the core of what you want and you can trust that they'll push back on certain things or add ideas in certain ways because they get the foundation.

358
00:25:18,960 --> 00:25:31,520
100% and they're excited about it, which is, you know, as you kind of interact with more and more people you work with as you go through life, you realize like excitement and heart and energy to work on a thing is more than fifty percent of it.

359
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:41,200
So someone who's excited about it enough and gets it enough to start like taking ownership of it and trying to like help you make it better is the person you want to work with.

360
00:25:41,360 --> 00:25:46,880
Even if there's someone who has four or five more years of experience will do it for the same price or the same whatever.

361
00:25:47,840 --> 00:25:49,360
Who just wants the to implement it.

362
00:25:49,600 --> 00:25:50,480
Yeah, that makes sense.

363
00:25:50,800 --> 00:25:52,480
Okay, so then you got that bill.

364
00:25:52,640 --> 00:25:57,280
What was, what would you say your role then was in the development of Weatherline?

365
00:25:57,680 --> 00:26:00,960
Was it like design and project management?

366
00:26:01,600 --> 00:26:04,240
Or was that sort of a shared role with him?

367
00:26:04,320 --> 00:26:06,640
Obviously he was at the implementation part.

368
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:12,800
Yeah, so this was, there was a lot of impostor syndrome at this time, right?

369
00:26:13,120 --> 00:26:21,520
Because probably no one remembers but me, but like, or, you know, probably no one, I'm the only one that has stuck in my head, like, hey, dude, do you need to go to therapy over this?

370
00:26:21,680 --> 00:26:24,960
But like everyone online was talking about how project management was.

371
00:26:25,840 --> 00:26:28,080
or product managers was like, that's not a thing.

372
00:26:28,240 --> 00:26:29,120
Like, you're an idiot.

373
00:26:29,280 --> 00:26:30,000
Like, that's not a job.

374
00:26:30,720 --> 00:26:36,080
There was this everyone wanted a flat organization where engineers talk directly to the CEO.

375
00:26:36,400 --> 00:26:39,440
And if you're not a CEO who has an engineering background, you're going to fail.

376
00:26:39,600 --> 00:26:40,800
Like, it was very like, Yeah.

377
00:26:41,200 --> 00:26:45,600
We're the software engineers, only us, only we can do this kind of outsider, insider thing.

378
00:26:46,080 --> 00:26:47,120
Taking the adults out of the room.

379
00:26:47,360 --> 00:26:49,680
Yeah, app director is the thing that people would throw around.

380
00:26:49,840 --> 00:26:54,800
Like, there's no, like, people would refer to me as an app director, kind of like a film director analogy.

381
00:26:55,280 --> 00:27:01,120
And there was a whole portion of the mobile and engineer world who was like, that's not a a thing.

382
00:27:01,520 --> 00:27:06,000
You're just an annoying person over our shoulder who can't contribute and would just ch chat on it.

383
00:27:06,560 --> 00:27:11,120
So I was, I had that imposture syndrome of like, okay, I need to learn how to code.

384
00:27:11,200 --> 00:27:12,720
The only way to add value is to learn how to code.

385
00:27:12,800 --> 00:27:14,640
Otherwise, I'm just staring over someone's shoulder.

386
00:27:14,800 --> 00:27:26,800
So yeah, to your question, I was doing, it was, you know, design, business, marketing, all the iterations through, you know, people don't realize how much in hand iteration goes into an app.

387
00:27:27,200 --> 00:27:30,880
And to be honest, what I had to start with with Weatherline was pretty basic.

388
00:27:31,040 --> 00:27:32,320
Like, I had the three screens.

389
00:27:32,720 --> 00:27:36,800
And beyond that, it was like, we're going to explore this together and see how things work..

390
00:27:37,040 --> 00:27:37,120
Yeah.

391
00:27:37,440 --> 00:27:43,280
So, you know, we would like, for example, we would figure out, oh, we couldn't do a 14 day forecast.

392
00:27:43,360 --> 00:27:44,800
We have to do a 7 day forecast.

393
00:27:44,880 --> 00:27:47,200
And then there's a lot of stuff that goes into it.

394
00:27:47,360 --> 00:27:49,680
It's like, okay, what are all the possible icon sets?

395
00:27:49,840 --> 00:27:51,200
Okay, how do I make good icons for that?

396
00:27:51,360 --> 00:27:52,480
Okay, how do they look on a line?

397
00:27:52,800 --> 00:28:01,920
And, you know, it's funny, you go look at Weatherline now, and I would say, hopefully, that it just kind of all works together, but it took so much iteration to learn.

398
00:28:02,240 --> 00:28:06,960
Like, if you go look at the icons, they're basically, what's the word when it's just like a line drawing?

399
00:28:07,600 --> 00:28:08,480
I mean, like outline?

400
00:28:08,720 --> 00:28:09,920
Yeah, kind of like an outline.

401
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:11,680
Like they're not multiple colors.

402
00:28:12,480 --> 00:28:14,960
They don't sit on a white background.

403
00:28:15,120 --> 00:28:16,400
They kind of all blend in the line.

404
00:28:16,560 --> 00:28:20,240
Like it took three or four iterations to learn that that's kind of the only way to do it.

405
00:28:20,480 --> 00:28:21,200
All those kinds of things.

406
00:28:21,280 --> 00:28:23,120
And then certain shapes work really well on a line.

407
00:28:23,360 --> 00:28:34,240
So like if there's like a horizontal design component of like a sun, like let's say it's like six rays and it's horizontal, then it's always going to look weird because the line is probably going through the middle of the horizontal line.

408
00:28:34,400 --> 00:28:36,080
So you have to get away from all those kinds of things.

409
00:28:36,240 --> 00:28:41,360
So I was sketching those and then getting a designer to make them pixel perfect.

410
00:28:42,240 --> 00:28:45,360
So I was doing all that kind of in the weeds stuff.

411
00:28:45,680 --> 00:28:45,680
Yeah.

412
00:28:46,320 --> 00:28:53,360
Well, and that's we've talked about a it a bunch of in the show, especially with where there's like it's the one person, you know, indie shop kind of deal.

413
00:28:53,920 --> 00:29:00,080
It's really hard to have the perspective of not being the person in the weeds.

414
00:29:00,480 --> 00:29:02,560
Like in the weeds meaning in the code itself.

